WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.730
Threat hunting is a proactive,

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:05.720
analyst-driven search for adversary activity that may not have produced a clear alert.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.560
A hunter starts from a defensive question, a hypothesis,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.280
or a known behavior pattern and then looks for evidence in available telemetry.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.010
The work is structured investigation,

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.740
not casual browsing through logs. It is also not a promise that something malicious is

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.280
present.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:23.000
A hunt can confirm suspicious activity, reject a weak idea, reveal telemetry gaps,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.535
or produce a better detection.

00:00:24.535 --> 00:00:26.490
Threat hunting is different from alert triage.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.690
In triage, the system has already created an alert,

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:32.570
and the analyst decides whether it is benign, suspicious, or incident-worthy.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:33.325
In hunting,

00:00:33.325 --> 00:00:37.040
the analyst is looking for activity that existing alerts may have missed or did not

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.675
classify clearly.

00:00:38.675 --> 00:00:40.720
Hunting is also different from incident response.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.950
Incident response begins when there is enough evidence to treat the situation as a

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.975
possible or confirmed incident.

00:00:45.975 --> 00:00:47.665
Hunting may feed incident response,

00:00:47.665 --> 00:00:51.760
but it usually begins earlier and with more uncertainty. Hunting is not vulnerability

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.216
scanning.

00:00:52.216 --> 00:00:56.570
A scanner asks whether assets have known weaknesses. A hunt asks whether behavior in the

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.825
environment suggests suspicious or adversary activity.

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:01.940
Hunting is not compliance review either.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.260
Compliance work may check whether required controls exist,

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.080
while hunting tests operational visibility and asks what the telemetry shows.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.830
A good security program can include all of these activities,

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.085
but they answer different questions.

00:01:13.085 --> 00:01:16.370
In a SOC, hunting fits between detection engineering, alert operations,

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.840
threat intelligence, and incident response.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.225
Hunters use telemetry from endpoints, identity systems, DNS, cloud logs, proxy logs,

00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:24.395
and other sources.

00:01:24.395 --> 00:01:29.215
They may use ATT&CK-style behavior mapping, recent incident lessons, business context,

00:01:29.215 --> 00:01:31.780
or known environmental risks to decide where to look.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.500
When a hunt produces a useful pattern,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.280
detection engineers can turn it into repeatable logic.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.970
When a hunt finds suspicious evidence,

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:42.040
incident responders can take over with an escalation process. The defensive discipline

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:42.355
matters.

00:01:42.355 --> 00:01:46.255
A hunt should have authorization, scope, notes, and evidence handling expectations.

00:01:46.255 --> 00:01:50.210
It should define the environment, time window, data sources, assumptions,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.220
and decision criteria. Hunters should avoid overclaiming.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.060
Unusual is not automatically malicious,

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.740
and absence of evidence is not proof that nothing happened.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.620
A practical hunt produces a documented outcome: evidence found, evidence not found,

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:07.005
a refined hypothesis, a telemetry gap, a tuned alert, or a new detection idea.

00:02:07.005 --> 00:02:10.030
That is what makes hunting valuable as an engineering practice rather than random

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:10.640
searching.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.435
A useful way to evaluate a hunt is to ask what decision it supports.

00:02:14.435 --> 00:02:17.830
The decision might be to escalate, close the hypothesis, improve a detection,

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:20.545
request better logging, or schedule a follow-up hunt.

00:02:20.545 --> 00:02:23.970
That decision focus keeps the work from becoming open-ended exploration.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.790
It also helps managers and other analysts understand why the hunt was worth doing even if

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:28.840
no incident was found.
